+# Epic RC1 Context: Applicant Attestation & Reference Capture
+
+<!-- Compiled from planning artifacts. Edit freely. Regenerate with compile-epic-context if planning docs change. -->
+
+## Goal
+
+The applicant reaches the References section of the application form, reads a
+plain-language explanation of what referee contact means, and makes an explicit
+Yes/No attestation choice — recorded honestly, with the fraud-comparator capture
+(IP/UA) taken at submission. This is the evidence foundation for all of
+reference-checking v1: Art 13 transparency (the applicant knows what will happen
+and has forewarned their referees) with legitimate interest as the lawful basis.
+
+## Stories
+
+- Story RC1.1: Attestation & capture schema + submission write path (done, PR #557)
+- Story RC1.2: Attestation UI — the References section choice
+
+## Requirements & Constraints
+
+- The choice is an explicit radio pair — **required, no default** — never an
+  opt-out checkbox; honest data, no assumed-yes dark pattern.
+- "Yes" (`attested`) writes a `reference_contact_attestation` row to
+  `acknowledgement_record` (current `policy_version`) inside the existing
+  `insert_acknowledgement_records` transaction; "No" (`declined`) writes NO row
+  and the application submits normally — no re-ask, no nag interstitial, no
+  score treatment (refusal is never scored against the applicant).
+- Choice is recorded per application on `application.reference_contact_choice`
+  (nullable TEXT, `'attested'`/`'declined'`/NULL) so later surfaces distinguish
+  "declined" from "pre-v1" (A4). Exposed on the detail payload; IP/UA never are
+  (internal-only fraud comparator).
+- Vocabulary is **attestation/acknowledgement — the word "consent" never
+  appears** in relation to this record in code, copy, or comments (AD-2).
+- Anti-coercion is a load-bearing promise: the trailing card line ("changes how
+  references are checked, not how your application is shown") must stay true.
+- WCAG 2.1 AA on the new surface; `data-testid`s on all interactive elements.
+
+## Technical Decisions
+
+- RC1.1 (done) built the entire write path: enum value, capture columns,
+  `validate_form` requires an explicit `attested`/`declined` choice (error copy
+  "Choose one option to continue."), insert-layer exhaustiveness guard (anything
+  else aborts the transaction — a silently coerced value would fabricate a
+  choice the applicant never made), IP/UA threaded from the request into the
+  insert, attestation row written only when attested.
+- Migrations are expand-only and idempotent; Squirrel workflow is mandatory for
+  SQL changes.
+- Shared type carries only `reference_contact_choice: Option(String)` with
+  absent-key-default-None decoding for pre-v1 cached payloads.
+
+## UX & Interaction Patterns
+
+- The attestation card reuses the `view_consent_block` anatomy (the existing
+  GDPR block): elevated card at the end of the References section with heading,
+  body copy, privacy link, radio pair, and anti-coercion footer — all copy
+  verbatim from the UX spec; never paraphrased.
+- References-section framing copy appears above the first referee field group;
+  the Yes-path confirmation page gains one added line; the No path shows nothing.
+- Validation follows the standard form pattern: inline on blur, scroll-to-block
+  on submit.
+- Design-system reuse only; brand voice (calm confidence, no exclamation marks).
+
+## Cross-Story Dependencies
+
+- RC1.2 renders the UI into RC1.1's plumbing — no new schema or write-path work.
+- RC1.2 ships BEFORE the form-stepper (`form-stepper-f1`): attestation
+  validation must stay simple and self-contained (one callable function) so the
+  stepper can lift it into its References-step gating on rebase.
+- RC3.7 reads the captured IP/UA via SQL directly for fraud-signal comparison.
+- The RC4 panel reads `reference_contact_choice` for its Off state.
